! ============================================================
! 02a  Basis-set convergence  (run all four, in order)
! ============================================================
! The SAME molecule and the SAME method, four basis sets.
! This is the single most important habit in the subject: a
! number from a quantum-chemistry program is meaningless until
! you know how it moves with the basis.
!
! Collect "Total energy" from all four runs:
!
!    basis        functions   E (Eh)          dE vs previous
!    STO-3G             7      -74.963018          --
!    6-31G(d)          19      -76.010505       -1.047
!    cc-pVDZ           25      -76.027113       -0.017
!    cc-pVTZ           65      -76.057681       -0.031
!
! TWO THINGS TO NOTICE:
!   * the energy falls MONOTONICALLY.  It must: the
!     variational principle guarantees that enlarging the
!     basis can only lower an HF energy.  If you ever see it
!     rise, something is wrong.
!   * it is still falling at cc-pVTZ.  The HF limit for water
!     is about -76.067 Eh, so even the largest basis here is
!     ~6 mEh short.  Absolute energies converge slowly;
!     differences converge much faster, which is why chemistry
!     is done with differences.
!
! Try the same series with %job opt and watch r(OH) converge
! to ~0.940 A -- geometries settle by cc-pVDZ, long before
! energies do.
%method  HF
%basis   6-31G(d)
%job     energy
%charge  0
%mult    1

%geo
 O   0.0000000   0.0000000   0.1173000
 H   0.0000000   0.7572000  -0.4692000
 H   0.0000000  -0.7572000  -0.4692000
